---
title: Progressive enhancement
description: Attach Superforms' enhance action and drive pending states, success messages and failure handling from its stores.
---

<script>
	import { Callout } from "$lib/markdown";
</script>

Enhancement is Superforms', not Formsnap's. The `Field` and `Control` markup does not change: attach `use:enhance` to the form and read the outcome from Superforms' stores.

## Attach enhance

```svelte title="src/routes/recipes/feedback/+page.svelte"
<script lang="ts">
	import { untrack } from "svelte";
	import { superForm } from "sveltekit-superforms";
	import { Control, Field, Label } from "@emmorts/formsnap";

	let { data } = $props();

	const form = superForm(untrack(() => data.form), { resetForm: false });
	const { form: formData, enhance, message, submitting } = form;
</script>

<form method="POST" use:enhance>
	<Field {form} name="answer" fieldErrors>
		<Control>
			{#snippet children({ props })}
				<Label>Answer</Label>
				<textarea {...props} bind:value={$formData.answer}></textarea>
			{/snippet}
		</Control>
	</Field>

	<button type="submit" disabled={$submitting}>
		{#if $submitting}Sending…{:else}Send answer{/if}
	</button>
</form>

{#if $message}
	<output>{$message}</output>
{/if}
```

The action picks the outcome: `fail(400, { form })` returns the posted values with the errors, and `message(form, "Answer received.")` reports success. Both are read from SvelteKit's page state, so no submission API is added on top of Superforms.

```ts title="src/routes/recipes/feedback/+page.server.ts"
import { fail } from "@sveltejs/kit";
import { message, superValidate } from "sveltekit-superforms";
import { zod } from "sveltekit-superforms/adapters";
import { answerSchema } from "./schema.js";

export const actions = {
	default: async ({ request }) => {
		const form = await superValidate(request, zod(answerSchema));
		if (!form.valid) return fail(400, { form });
		return message(form, "Answer received.");
	},
};
```

## Failure versus status

When one action serves both the enhanced and the native path, the two differ in transport:

| Path                  | Result                                                    |
| --------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |
| enhanced submission   | HTTP 200 with a failure `ActionResult` in the JSON body    |
| native POST (no JS)   | HTTP 400                                                   |

Assert the result's `type` and `status`; use SvelteKit's `deserialize` on the response body when you need the action's returned values.

<Callout type="warning">

Return **Superforms'** `fail`, not SvelteKit's, when the form can carry `File` values. Superforms' `fail` strips files from the result, which SvelteKit's serialization cannot encode — SvelteKit's own `fail` throws on them. See [File uploads](/docs/recipes/file-uploads).

</Callout>

## Pending and success feedback

`$submitting`, `$delayed`, `$timeout` and `$message` come from the same `superForm` instance. `$submitting` flips for the whole request; `$delayed` waits for Superforms' `delayMs` before turning true, which keeps fast requests from flashing a spinner.

```svelte
const { form: formData, enhance, submitting, delayed, timeout, message } = form;
```

```svelte
<button type="submit" disabled={$submitting}>
	{#if $delayed}Sending…{:else}Send answer{/if}
</button>
{#if $timeout}<p>Still working — the request is taking longer than expected.</p>{/if}
{#if $message}<output>{$message}</output>{/if}
```

## Form owner lifetime

The `form` instance passed to `Field`, `Fieldset` and `ElementField` is fixed for the mounted scope's lifetime. Updates, validation and resets on that instance stay reactive; replacing the instance requires remounting the whole scope, including nested fields and headless hooks.

```svelte
{#key currentForm}
	<Field form={currentForm} name="email">
		{#snippet children({ value })}
			<output>{value}</output>
		{/snippet}
	</Field>
{/key}
```

Key the parent `Field` or `Fieldset`, not just an `ElementField` inside it. Initialize the Superforms instances once — never call `superForm` reactively to replace them.

